From eadba10050cc34223a0ddd014f732d57f349c23e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:47:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): transcript cache SQLite runs on a worker
 thread, off the main loop [daveey]

The mini's Desktop main loop stalled 1764 ms and 1053 ms during a
tests/hermes_state CI job (2026-11-11 04:24Z / 04:27Z), each logged in
the same millisecond as a "transcript prefetch: 1 fetched" line.

Cause: TranscriptCacheStore is synchronous node:sqlite and ran on
Electron's main thread. The entry the first stall wrote
(macbook/local-b30ebd47..., 614 KB) carries saved_at 20:24:21.897, the
clock read at the top of write(); the lag line was logged at 23.516. So
1.62 s of that 1.76 s stall was inside write() (upsert, COMMIT with its
WAL auto-checkpoint, bounds check), with the host at 89 MB free memory
and the SSD saturated by the CI job. Replayed on a copy of the same
cache without that pressure, the whole putLatest takes 38.6 ms at worst
over all 1000 entries: the code path is cheap, the main thread was
waiting on the disk.

Fix: the store moves to a worker thread (transcript-cache-worker.ts,
bundled as dist/electron-transcript-cache-worker.js). The main process
only awaits it through transcript-cache-client.ts:
- prefetch: one batched metaMany per pass, awaited putLatest;
- renderer put/drop/clear/migrate: posted to the worker;
- renderer sync read: returnValue is set when the worker replies (1.5 s
  cap, then a cache miss). The renderer blocks on it either way; the
  main loop no longer does.
A worker that dies is restarted (3 starts), then the store falls back
to the main thread and says so in desktop.log. Ops slower than 250 ms
are logged with the thread they ran on. The lag-warning threshold is
unchanged.

Tests: a store op that blocks its thread for 1 s leaves the caller's
event loop turning (and the in-thread control stalls it); a prefetch
pass with 1 s writes does the same through the host; the real worker
bundle round-trips through SQLite; restart/fallback/timeout; the
prefetcher over an asynchronous store; the shipped bundle is emitted
and runs from its text alone.
